    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Is This a Good Offer?\n\n**It is a solid baseline L6 PM offer, but it is leaving money on the table for someone in your position.**\n\n* **The Good:** An initial equity grant of $1.08M over 4 years ($270k/yr average) is healthy for a Google L6 PM. Furthermore, Google's initial grants are typically **front-loaded (33% / 33% / 22% / 12%)**:\n  * **Year 1 TC:** $235k base + $47k bonus + $356.4k equity (33%) = **~$638k**\n  * **Year 2 TC:** $235k base + $47k bonus + $356.4k equity (33%) = **~$638k** *(+ any Year 1 refresher vest)*\n  * **Year 3 & 4 TC:** The initial grant drops sharply to 22% ($237.6k) in Year 3 and 12% ($129.6k) in Year 4, creating an \"equity cliff\" where ongoing total comp depends heavily on stacked performance refreshes.\n* **The Bad:** \n  * **Base salary is a step down:** At $235,000, you would take a slight cut from your current $240,000 base, despite their posted range reaching up to **$271,000**.\n  * **$0 Sign-on Bonus:** Google almost always has discretion to grant a first-year cash sign-on bonus for lateral senior hires, especially to bridge timing gaps or forfeit equity.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Decision: Accept, Walk Away, or Negotiate?\n\n**You should negotiate.**\n\nYou have the best possible negotiating leverage:\n1. **You are passively recruited:** You were not looking and are happy at Meta.\n2. **True peer-level parity:** A Meta IC6 to Google L6 is a lateral move where recruiters expect candidates to push for top-of-band terms.\n3. **No risk:** Big Tech companies like Google do not rescind offers simply because a candidate makes a polite, data-backed counteroffer.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What to Ask For (Your Counter-Offer)\n\n| Component | Google Initial Offer | Your Counter Target | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000 \u2013 $260,000** | Bridges the gap above your current $240k base; sits comfortably below the $271k posted ceiling. |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Compensates for the disruption of switching, unvested Meta RSUs, and bonus cycle timing. |\n| **Equity (4-Yr)** | $1,080,000 | **$1,250,000 \u2013 $1,300,000** | Lifts average annual equity to ~$312k\u2013$325k/yr, protecting against the Year 3/4 drop-off. |\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Frame the Counter to Your Recruiter\n\nWhen speaking with your recruiter, convey enthusiasm for the team and scope while remaining firm on the financial gap:\n\n> *\"Thank you for putting this offer together. I\u2019ve enjoyed meeting the team and am genuinely excited about the impact of the role.*\n>\n> *Because I wasn't actively looking and have a great trajectory and compensation at Meta (currently at $526k/yr with $240k base), leaving requires a package that clearly reflects a step forward. To make this an easy decision for me:*\n>\n> 1. * **Base Salary:** Since my current base is $240k, I'd like to get closer to the top of your range at **$255k\u2013$260k**.\n> 2. * **Equity:** To bridge my ongoing Meta vesting trajectory and account for outer-year compensation pacing, I\u2019d like to see the equity grant at **$1.25M\u2013$1.3M**.\n> 3. * **Sign-on Bonus:** Because there is no sign-on included and I will be walking away from upcoming bonus and equity milestones at Meta, a sign-on bonus of **$75k\u2013$100k** would make this a seamless transition.\n>\n> *If we can get the total package to this level, I am ready to sign and begin onboarding.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Key Takeaway\nGoogle will likely return with a compromise\u2014such as a base increase to ~$245k\u2013$250k, a $40k\u2013$60k sign-on bonus, and a slight bump in equity (to ~$1.15M\u2013$1.2M). Countering now will almost certainly result in a stronger compensation package with minimal downside risk.",
