     "content": "### 1. The Numbers Breakdown\n\nGoogle uses a **front-loaded vesting schedule (33% / 33% / 22% / 12%)** for GSUs. Here is how your revised compensation breaks down:\n\n| Component | Year 1 | Year 2 | Year 3 | Year 4 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $240,000 | $240,000 | $240,000 |\n| **Target Bonus (20%)** | $48,000 | $48,000 | $48,000 | $48,000 |\n| **Initial GSU Vest** | **$422,400** (33%) | **$422,400** (33%) | **$281,600** (22%) | **$153,600** (12%) |\n| **Refresher Grants** *(est.)* | $0 | ~$25,000 | ~$60,000 | ~$110,000 |\n| **Projected Total Comp** | **~$710,400** | **~$735,400** | **~$630,000** | **~$550,000+** |\n\n#### Comparison to Meta:\n* **Your Current Meta TC:** ~$526,000/yr (with ~$200k vesting over the next 12 months).\n* **Google Year 1 TC:** **~$710,400** (an immediate **+$184,000 / ~35% jump**).\n* **Unvested Meta RSUs:** The extra equity at Google in Year 1 alone ($422.4k vs. $200k at Meta) completely absorbs the $200k you leave behind, while giving you an identical base salary and a slightly higher target bonus.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Is This Top of Band?\n\n**Yes.** A **$1.28M initial equity grant** for an L6 PM is at the very top of Google's standard hiring bands. \n\nBy raising the equity by $200,000 and matching your $240,000 base, the recruiter went through the compensation committee to secure a genuine concession. When recruiters say *\"this is our best offer\"* after already conceding a ~$220k total value increase, they have almost certainly hit the L6 ceiling. Pushing further is unlikely to yield results and risks deal fatigue.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Should You Take It?\n\nSince compensation matters significantly to you, this offer is strong enough to justify leaving. However, because you are happy at Meta, evaluate the choice using these criteria:\n\n#### Accept the Offer If:\n1. **You want the immediate ~$350,000+ financial premium across Years 1 & 2:** Earning >$700k/yr as an L6 without managing managers is rare in Big Tech.\n2. **You want FAANG/MAMAA diversification:** Having both Meta (IC6) and Google (L6) on your resume gives you career optionality at the Staff/Principal level.\n3. **You prefer Google\u2019s PM culture:** Google PM roles tend to focus heavily on strategy, platform scale, and cross-functional consensus, often with better work-life balance compared to Meta's aggressive, metric-driven execution cycles.\n\n#### Stay at Meta If:\n1. **You are close to an IC7 (Director/Principal PM) promotion:** Resetting at Google means building social capital from scratch, which typically delays your next promotion by 1.5\u20132 years. If an IC7 promo at Meta is within 12 months, that level jump could outpace Google\u2019s L6 compensation in Years 3 and 4.\n2. **You prefer Meta's product pace:** If you thrive in high-autonomy, ship-fast environments, Google\u2019s consensus-heavy approval and launch processes can feel slow.\n\n---\n\n### Final Verdict\n\n**Take the offer.** You successfully negotiated a **top-of-band L6 package** that pays you over **$710k in Year 1**, fully covers your forfeited Meta equity, and establishes you at the senior level in both major tech ecosystems.",
